#!/bin/bash
# cc-await-ping — block until a NEW line lands in this session's mailbox, print it,
# and exit. The modal-safe PULL complement to cc-notify's push.
#
# Launch it via Bash(run_in_background) before going idle after a `--notify-back`
# handoff: when the fired peer pings (a mailbox write), this exits and the harness's
# task-completion notification re-invokes you — even if the peer's composer injection
# mislanded because you were at a permission/modal prompt. The mailbox write is the
# wake; the injection is the interrupt. Both paths, one loop closed.
#
#   cc-await-ping [<uuid>]          default uuid = own pane ($ITERM_SESSION_ID)
#
# Options:
#   --role <name>   watch the ROLE's mailbox (~/.claude/cc-roles/<name>), re-resolved
#                   EVERY poll cycle — follows a recycle that re-points the role file.
#                   On a mid-wait switch the new mailbox baselines at 0 (never miss the
#                   ping; a rare replay-wake is benign). Mutually exclusive with <uuid>.
#   --idle-scoped   THE ONE SHAPE THAT IS SAFE UNDER A LIVE /goal (see § IDLE-SCOPED below):
#                   additionally terminates on any NEW TURN of its own session, so the
#                   deferral it creates spans exactly the idle window. Defaults --timeout
#                   3600 and --interval 5 (both still overridable).
#   --sid <id>      this session's Claude session id — the key of its turn-boundary beat
#                   (~/.claude/cc-beats/<sid>.json). Required by --idle-scoped in practice;
#                   omitted, the mode resolves it by pane and REFUSES if it cannot.
#   --timeout <s>   give up after S seconds (default 1800; exit 2)
#   --interval <s>  poll cadence in seconds (default 15)
#   -h, --help
#
# Watches EVERY key of its inbox, not just the one it was handed: cc-notify writes the PANE box and
# mailbox-drain.sh reads the SESSION box, so a single-key watcher can sit deaf beside a delivered line
# (lib → mailbox_keyset). Hence the no-arg form is the one to advertise — it derives the same key
# cc-notify resolves, then covers the rest.
#
# EVERY exit says how long the watch actually held. Each verdict line on stderr carries
# `verdict=<token> elapsed=<MEASURED wall seconds>s budget=<the --timeout you asked for>s`, and the
# timeout path adds `term=full|short|unknown`. elapsed is a delta from a start stamp (see T0 below),
# NEVER the configured value — it used to be $TIMEOUT, so a watcher that collapsed instantly and one
# that held its term printed the identical string, and "was my wake path real, or was I deaf?" could
# not be answered from this tool's own output (backlog e2903b01dfdc). `term=short` is the answer to
# that question when it is bad news: the watch retired before its budget and you were deaf for the
# rest. `elapsed=unknown` = no usable clock; never read it as a full term.
#
# Exit: 0 = new line(s) arrived (printed to stdout; `verdict=ping` on stderr); 2 = timed out — with
#           `term=full` the DESIGNED end of a watch that ran its term, echoed as `verdict=timeout` on
#           stderr because the harness labels every non-zero background exit "failed" (see the exit
#           path at the foot of this file);
#       3 = no uuid/role;
#       4 = line(s) DELIVERED but the shared cursor could not be advanced (F9 — you WILL see this mail
#           again; that is a dup, not a loss). Loud on purpose: a cursor-write failure that exits 0 is
#           indistinguishable from a clean take.
#       5 = ORPHANED — the session that armed this watcher is provably gone. Exits WITHOUT consuming,
#           leaving the mail unacked (see the owner guard below for why that is the whole point).
#       6 = REFUSED TO ARM (--idle-scoped only) — a precondition of the safe shape does not hold, so
#           NOTHING was parked and no deferral was created. `verdict=refused reason=<token>` on
#           stderr names which one and what to do instead. Refusal is the SAFE direction here: the
#           mode's whole licence to park under a live /goal is that it self-cancels, so a mode that
#           cannot prove it will self-cancel must not park at all.
#
# ── § IDLE-SCOPED — why this mode exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# docs/research/goal-safe-2way-comms-2026-08-13.md §§3-4. A session has two idle modes and needs a
# third. With a background task parked, CC defers /goal evaluation indefinitely (the STARVATION pole:
# measured 47 of 84 goal sessions never evaluated once); with the registry quiet, an unmet goal blocks
# every stop until the cap (the SPIN pole: one session burned 90 unmet evaluations in 76 minutes
# re-judging a world in which nothing had changed). Neither is idle.
#
# The missing primitive is an awaiter whose deferral is IDLE-SCOPED — it dies on peer mail AND on any
# new turn of its own session. Then CC's deferral (which is correct: A2) spans exactly the window in
# which nothing has changed, and the goal is judged ONCE PER NEW-INFORMATION EVENT rather than 90
# times while waiting or 0 times forever. Measured economics say that is where all the verdict value
# lives: 82% of met goals were met on evaluation #1, and the met rate falls to 27% at ≥10.
#
# The mode is therefore two exit conditions and a fail-closed arm gate:
#   C1 exit-on-mail   unchanged — the wake half of 2-way comms.
#   C2 exit-on-turn   a turn boundary newer than the arm turn's OWN trailing Stop ⇒ the session was
#                     woken by something else, so this awaiter's job is over: exit 0 SILENTLY
#                     (verdict=stood-down), leaving the next natural Stop registry-quiet. Stated as
#                     "a UserPromptSubmit-kind beat newer than the baseline" in the design doc, which
#                     is not implementable as written: the arm is instructed through a Stop-hook
#                     block, and such a continuation turn fires no UserPromptSubmit at all, so its
#                     only beat is a Stop (see THE ALLOWANCE below — backlog b60eb29e97dd).
#   C3 single-instance   refuse while a live sibling watcher claims any key of the keyset.
#   C4 refuse-on-pending refuse while mail is already pending — mail waiting means you have work, and
#                     arming would defer the judgment of state you ALREADY hold.
#   C5 bounded        --timeout defaults to 3600 here (not 14400): the timeout exit is itself a task
#                     completion, so it is the "re-look at the world" floor and the in-session
#                     deadline check for a peer that will never ping.
# The arm gate additionally refuses when the beat oracle C2 depends on cannot be read (unresolvable
# sid, absent or stale beat file, no mailbox lib). That refusal is the mode's keystone, not a
# nicety: an idle-scoped watcher that cannot self-cancel IS the starvation pole, armed deliberately
# and blessed by a flag.
#
# Env overrides (tests): CC_BEAT_DIR · CC_AWAIT_BEAT_FRESH_S (arm-time beat staleness bound, default
# 900s) — plus the CC_MAILBOX_DIR / CC_ROLES_DIR / CC_COMMS_ALARM_DIR / CC_REGISTRY_DIR seams below.
#
# CODES THE HARNESS SYNTHESIZES — NOT OURS, AND NOT 128+signal. If you see this watcher reported as
# `failed with exit code 143` or `144`, it never reached any exit above; it was SIGTERMed, and the
# number tells you WHAT was hit, because the harness's child is the zsh wrapper, not this script:
#   143 = the WATCHER process was TERMed; zsh survived and exited with its own 128+15 status.
#   144 = the whole process GROUP was TERMed (the wrapper died) — a harness *sentinel*, not arithmetic.
# 144 also proves the SIGTERM came from OUTSIDE the harness: every harness-initiated kill resolves the
# task with its own code BEFORE signalling, so it can never surface as 144. Do NOT read 144 as 128+16
# ⇒ SIGURG — that reading is arithmetically tempting, self-refuting (SIGURG's default action is IGNORE,
# so bash cannot die from it), and it cost one investigation its suspect. An exit code cannot name a
# sender; capture one with an SA_SIGINFO recorder (si_pid). Measured 2026-08-07, CC 2.1.220 —
# docs/research/await-ping-exit-144-2026-08-07.md.
# Env overrides (tests): CC_MAILBOX_DIR, CC_ROLES_DIR, CC_COMMS_ALARM_DIR, CC_REGISTRY_DIR.
set -uo pipefail

# ── MEASURED, NEVER CONFIGURED (backlog e2903b01dfdc) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every verdict in this file reports `elapsed=` as a WALL-CLOCK DELTA from this stamp. It used to
# print `elapsed=${TIMEOUT}s` — the value the CALLER PASSED IN — on the timeout path, which made the
# one question an originator of a `--notify-back` wave actually needs answerable unanswerable from the
# tool's own output: *was my wake path real, or was I deaf?* A watcher that collapsed instantly
# printed the identical string as one that held for four hours, and the field is named `elapsed`, so
# it READ as evidence the watch ran its term. MEASURED 2026-08-11: `--timeout 20` returned rc 2 after
# 21 s wall — blocking IS genuine today and the label merely coincided. A coincidence is not
# instrumentation, and backlog #127 records a REAL early-exit mode (exit 144 — the armed wake path
# silently disarms), which is exactly the case the old string could not distinguish.
#
# Stamped HERE, before arg parsing and before the owner-guard oracles, so the delta covers the
# watcher's WHOLE life — the same discipline scripts/worktree-gc.sh:135 stamps its own `elapsed=` by.
# `date +%s`, not bash's SECONDS: bash 3.2-safe and unambiguously wall clock (this file already
# shells out to `date` on every alarm path, so the fork is not new).
T0="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
case "$T0" in ''|*[!0-9]*) T0=0 ;; esac
_elapsed_s() {   # MEASURED wall seconds since the stamp; -1 = unmeasurable (never a guess)
  local now
  [ "$T0" -gt 0 ] || { echo -1; return 0; }
  now="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  case "$now" in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo -1; return 0 ;; esac
  [ "$now" -ge "$T0" ] || { echo -1; return 0; }   # clock stepped backwards ⇒ say unknown, never lie
  echo "$(( now - T0 ))"
}
# The field its readers grep. `elapsed=unknown` when no clock could answer — an unmeasurable duration
# reported as a number is the defect this whole block exists to remove, so it is never printed as one.
_elapsed_f() {
  local e; e="$(_elapsed_s)"
  if [ "$e" -lt 0 ]; then echo "elapsed=unknown"; else echo "elapsed=${e}s"; fi
}

MAILBOX_DIR="${CC_MAILBOX_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/mailbox}"
ROLES_DIR="${CC_ROLES_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/cc-roles}"
TIMEOUT=1800
INTERVAL=15
UUID=""
ROLE=""
IDLE_SCOPED=0
SID=""
_TIMEOUT_SET=0
_INTERVAL_SET=0

usage() { sed -n '2,/^# Watches EVERY key/p' "$0" | sed '$d' | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; }

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --role)         ROLE="${2:?--role needs a value}"; shift 2 ;;
    --timeout)      TIMEOUT="${2:?--timeout needs a value}"; _TIMEOUT_SET=1; shift 2 ;;
    --interval)     INTERVAL="${2:?--interval needs a value}"; _INTERVAL_SET=1; shift 2 ;;
    --idle-scoped)  IDLE_SCOPED=1; shift ;;
    --sid)          SID="${2:?--sid needs a value}"; shift 2 ;;
    -h|--help)      usage; exit 0 ;;
    --*)            echo "cc-await-ping: unknown option '$1'" >&2; usage >&2; exit 2 ;;
    *)              if [ -z "$UUID" ]; then UUID="$1"; shift; else echo "cc-await-ping: too many arguments" >&2; exit 2; fi ;;
  esac
done

if [ -n "$ROLE" ] && [ -n "$UUID" ]; then
  echo "cc-await-ping: --role and <uuid> are mutually exclusive" >&2; exit 2
fi

# C5: the mode's own defaults, applied ONLY where the caller did not state one. 3600 rather than the
# 14400 the bare form is nagged with, because here the timeout is a FEATURE — its exit is a task
# completion, i.e. a periodic "re-look at the world" and the in-session deadline check for a peer
# that will never ping. 5s rather than 15s because the added poll is one stat of a small beat file,
# and the interval is what bounds the known race (a wake turn that ends before the next poll costs
# exactly one bounce turn, never a periodic cost).
if [ "$IDLE_SCOPED" = 1 ]; then
  [ "$_TIMEOUT_SET" = 1 ]  || TIMEOUT="${CC_AWAIT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S:-3600}"
  [ "$_INTERVAL_SET" = 1 ] || INTERVAL="${CC_AWAIT_IDLE_INTERVAL_S:-5}"
fi
if [ "$IDLE_SCOPED" = 0 ] && [ -n "$SID" ]; then
  echo "cc-await-ping: --sid is only meaningful with --idle-scoped (the beat oracle is that mode's exit condition)" >&2; exit 2
fi

resolve_uuid() {
  # role mode: re-read the role file each call (follows recycles); else the fixed uuid.
  if [ -n "$ROLE" ]; then
    local rf="$ROLES_DIR/$ROLE"
    [ -f "$rf" ] || { echo ""; return; }
    tr -d '[:space:]' < "$rf"
  else
    echo "$UUID"
  fi
}

if [ -z "$ROLE" ] && [ -z "$UUID" ]; then
  _it="${CC_PANE_ID:-${ITERM_SESSION_ID:-}}"; UUID="${_it##*:}"
fi
CUR_UUID="$(resolve_uuid)"
[ -n "$CUR_UUID" ] || { echo "cc-await-ping: no uuid given and no \$ITERM_SESSION_ID (or role file '$ROLES_DIR/$ROLE' missing)" >&2; exit 3; }

# ── cursor lib (critique F6a, amended by F-3 2026-08-09): SEED off the `.seen` cursor the drain/fold
#    use, so mail ALREADY pending when we arm fires IMMEDIATELY — the old `wc -l` arm-time baseline
#    missed exactly that line. But seed ONCE and then track a PRIVATE cursor: polling `.seen` live is
#    what let the drain starve this watcher (see the F-3 block above the main loop). On fire,
#    mailbox_take_from advances seen+acked without regressing them (our stdout rides the harness
#    task-completion notification → a reliable delivery the model reads). Lib absent → the standalone
#    grep -c '' fallback, which is arrival-based and therefore un-starvable too.
_bd="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)"
_mbxlib="$_bd/../hooks/lib/mailbox-pending.sh"
[ -f "$_mbxlib" ] || _mbxlib="$HOME/.claude/hooks/lib/mailbox-pending.sh"
_have_lib=0
if [ -f "$_mbxlib" ] && command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  # mailbox_take_from is REQUIRED, not optional: it is what makes this watcher's trigger private (F-3
  # below). An older deployed lib without it degrades to the standalone line-count baseline — which is
  # arrival-based and therefore also un-starvable, merely blind to mail already pending at arm time —
  # rather than calling a function that does not exist.
  # shellcheck source=/dev/null
  . "$_mbxlib" 2>/dev/null && command -v mailbox_take_from >/dev/null 2>&1 && _have_lib=1
fi

# comms-alarm chokepoint lib. $0 is resolved THROUGH its symlinks first: ~/.claude/bin/cc-await-ping
# is a per-file symlink into the checkout, so a bare dirname "$0" finds only the DEPLOYED hooks/lib,
# which does not hold a brand-new tracked file until install.sh re-globs it.
_ca_self="$0"
while [ -L "$_ca_self" ]; do
  _ca_l="$(readlink "$_ca_self")"
  if [ "${_ca_l#/}" != "$_ca_l" ]; then _ca_self="$_ca_l"; else _ca_self="$(dirname "$_ca_self")/$_ca_l"; fi
done
_ca_root="$(cd "$(dirname "$_ca_self")/.." 2>/dev/null && pwd || true)"
for _ca_lib in "$_ca_root/hooks/lib/comms-alarm.sh" "$_bd/../hooks/lib/comms-alarm.sh" "$HOME/.claude/hooks/lib/comms-alarm.sh"; do
  if [ -f "$_ca_lib" ]; then
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    . "$_ca_lib" 2>/dev/null && command -v comms_alarm_write >/dev/null 2>&1 && break
  fi
done

MBOX="$MAILBOX_DIR/$CUR_UUID.md"
_lc() { if [ -f "$MBOX" ]; then grep -c '' "$MBOX" 2>/dev/null; else echo 0; fi; }   # grep -c '' — matches the lib (F1)
baseline="$(_lc)"
_banner="cc-await-ping: watching $CUR_UUID inbox ($([ "$_have_lib" = 1 ] && echo 'private cursor, seeded from .seen' || echo "from line $baseline"); timeout ${TIMEOUT}s, every ${INTERVAL}s)"
# Held back in idle-scoped mode until the arm gate has passed: announcing a watch that is then
# REFUSED tells its reader the opposite of what happened, and this stderr is the one stream the
# harness's task notification carries.
[ "$IDLE_SCOPED" = 1 ] || echo "$_banner" >&2

# Wake-path HEARTBEAT (critique F5): touch <uuid>.watching each poll so cc-notify/cc-announce can tell a
# live session that WILL be woken (a fresh watcher heartbeat) from a live-but-idle one that only drains on
# its next turn. Cleaned on exit. Works for uuid- AND role-watchers (both resolve to a concrete uuid).
#
# PROVEN WAKE (not merely a claimed one): the heartbeat now carries the WATCHER'S PID, so the claim it
# makes is FALSIFIABLE by its readers. An empty marker asserted "a watcher exists" and could only be
# checked for freshness — so a watcher killed by SIGKILL (no EXIT trap, marker left behind) kept earning
# "wake-path armed" for a full CC_WATCH_FRESH_S window, and cc-announce stamped VERIFIED on a wake that
# could not happen. Recording the pid lets cc-notify / mailbox-drain require it to be ALIVE. Format is
# one `pid=<n>` line; a legacy/empty marker still reads as armed-by-freshness, so nothing regresses.
#
# BEATEN UNDER EVERY KEY OF THE INBOX (2026-07-31), for the same reason the take below reads every
# key: the two hooks that ask "is this session armed?" ask about DIFFERENT keys — mailbox-drain.sh
# passes its session id (:170) and session-continue.sh passes the canonicalised key (:412) — so a
# single-key marker answers one of them and not the other, and the hook that misses re-nudges a
# session that is in fact armed. One marker per key makes the question key-agnostic.
_keys() {   # every physical key of this logical inbox (see the lib's mailbox_keyset)
  if [ "$_have_lib" = 1 ] && command -v mailbox_keyset >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    mailbox_keyset "$CUR_UUID" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s\n' "$CUR_UUID"
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$CUR_UUID"   # no lib ⇒ no alias to resolve ⇒ exactly the single-key behaviour
  fi
}
BEAT_KEYS="$CUR_UUID"
#
# ONE MARKER, MANY WATCHERS (backlog def25802babf, measured 2026-08-08). The marker is a SINGLE FILE
# per key with ONE owner slot, but a session may legitimately have TWO watchers armed on that key at
# once — the ordinary re-arm, where a fresh watcher is started near the term-end of the old one. The
# exiting one used to `rm` the key unconditionally, so a live sibling's heartbeat vanished and the
# "is this session armed?" hooks reported DEAF while a watcher was genuinely beating: watcher A timed
# out at 18:44 and cleared key …843 while B (pid 58128) was alive and re-stamping the marker every
# 15s. A false DEAF downgrades a real wake path in cc-notify / cc-announce for up to one full poll.
#
# WHY A pid==$$ GUARD ALONE IS NOT THE FIX — it is only the fallback layer below. Both watchers write
# the SAME file every poll, so at A's exit the slot holds whichever of them beat MOST RECENTLY, which
# is arbitrary. In the half of the overlaps where it holds A's pid, both readings are wrong: deleting
# blanks B's claim, and keeping leaves a marker naming a pid that is about to be DEAD — and
# mailbox_wake_armed reads a dead pid as NOT armed (that is the whole point of recording it). Either
# way the session reads deaf until B's next beat. The ownership question the slot cannot answer is
# "does another live watcher exist for this key?", so each watcher also stakes a per-pid CLAIM file
# and the exiting one HANDS THE SLOT OVER to a live claimant instead of deleting it.
#
# Claims are trusted only while FRESH (a live watcher re-stamps its claim every poll), on the same
# CC_WATCH_FRESH_S window its readers judge the marker by — so a stale claim cannot resurrect a
# marker its readers would already call dead, and a recycled pid cannot inherit a dead watcher's
# claim. A sibling running a version with no claim file still survives via the pid guard.
WATCH_CLAIM_DIR="$MAILBOX_DIR/.watchers"
_claim_live() {   # <key> → print the pid of a LIVE OTHER watcher beating this key; rc 1 = none
  local k="$1" c p mt now
  now="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  for c in "$WATCH_CLAIM_DIR/$k".*; do
    [ -e "$c" ] || continue                       # unmatched glob expands to itself — never a claim
    p="${c##*.}"
    case "$p" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
    [ "$p" = "$$" ] && continue                   # my own claim proves nothing about a survivor
    # GNU FIRST, then BSD — the reverse of the idiom used elsewhere in this repo, because that order
    # is silently WRONG on GNU rather than merely unportable: `stat -f %m <file>` on coreutils reads
    # `-f` as --file-system and `%m` as a FILE, so it prints a filesystem block to stdout and exits
    # 1. The `||` fallback then runs and APPENDS the epoch to that block, so the substitution yields
    # multi-line garbage, the `*[!0-9]*` guard below clamps it to 0, and every claim reads STALE —
    # i.e. C3 would refuse nothing at all. `stat -c` is an illegal option on BSD (rc 1, no stdout),
    # so this order degrades cleanly in the direction the other does not. Measured both ways.
    mt="$(stat -c %Y "$c" 2>/dev/null || stat -f %m "$c" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    case "$mt" in ''|*[!0-9]*) mt=0 ;; esac
    if [ "$(( now - mt ))" -le "${CC_WATCH_FRESH_S:-90}" ] && kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null; then
      printf '%s\n' "$p"; return 0
    fi
    kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null || rm -f "$c" 2>/dev/null || true   # its watcher is gone: claims nothing
  done
  return 1
}
_beat() {
  local k
  BEAT_KEYS="$(_keys)"
  mkdir -p "$WATCH_CLAIM_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true
  for k in $BEAT_KEYS; do
    printf 'pid=%s\n' "$$" > "$MAILBOX_DIR/$k.watching" 2>/dev/null || true
    : > "$WATCH_CLAIM_DIR/$k.$$" 2>/dev/null || true
  done
}
# Clears exactly the markers this process still OWNS — never a key it merely resolved once, and never
# one a live sibling is maintaining. bash 3.2, default IFS: the unquoted expansion splits the
# newline-separated set (this file is #!/bin/bash, not zsh).
_unbeat() {
  local k sib owner
  for k in $BEAT_KEYS; do
    rm -f "$WATCH_CLAIM_DIR/$k.$$" 2>/dev/null || true
    if sib="$(_claim_live "$k")"; then
      # HAND OVER, do not delete: the survivor keeps the wake path, and the slot names a LIVE pid
      # immediately rather than after up to one of its poll intervals.
      printf 'pid=%s\n' "$sib" > "$MAILBOX_DIR/$k.watching" 2>/dev/null || true
      continue
    fi
    owner="$(sed -n 's/^pid=\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p' "$MAILBOX_DIR/$k.watching" 2>/dev/null | head -n1)"
    # No pid recorded ⇒ legacy/empty marker ⇒ unchanged behaviour (removed), exactly as before: an
    # empty marker still reads as armed-by-freshness, so leaving one behind would advertise a wake
    # path nobody provides — the stale claim this file's PROVEN WAKE note exists to prevent.
    if [ -n "$owner" ] && [ "$owner" != "$$" ] && kill -0 "$owner" 2>/dev/null; then continue; fi
    rm -f "$MAILBOX_DIR/$k.watching" 2>/dev/null || true
  done
}
# ── THE IDLE-SCOPED ARM GATE — refuse rather than park, whenever the safe shape cannot be proven ──
# Placed BEFORE `trap '_unbeat' EXIT` deliberately: a refusal must park nothing and disturb no
# marker, so it must not run the EXIT cleanup of a watcher that never existed.
#
# THE THREE REFUSALS ARE NOT SYMMETRIC WITH THE EXITS. An exit is a watch that did its job; a
# refusal is a watch that was never safe to start. They are separated by an exit code (6) because
# their READER differs: an exit re-invokes the model with a result, whereas a refusal re-invokes it
# with an instruction about the state it is actually in. Collapsing them would make "your peer
# answered" and "you already have unread mail" the same event.
BEAT_DIR="${CC_BEAT_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/cc-beats}"
BEAT_FRESH_S="${CC_AWAIT_BEAT_FRESH_S:-900}"
BEAT_BASE_SEQ=0
BEAT_STOP_ALLOWANCE=0

_beat_field() {  # <sid> <jq-path> → field value on stdout; empty on any failure
  local s="$1" p="$2"
  [ -n "$s" ] || return 1
  [ -f "$BEAT_DIR/$s.json" ] || return 1
  jq -r "$p // empty" "$BEAT_DIR/$s.json" 2>/dev/null
}

_resolve_sid() {  # --sid > $CC_AWAIT_SID > the freshest beat whose pane is OURS; empty = unresolvable
  local pane f p t best_t=0 best=""
  [ -n "$SID" ] && { printf '%s' "$SID"; return 0; }
  if [ -n "${CC_AWAIT_SID:-}" ]; then printf '%s' "$CC_AWAIT_SID"; return 0; fi
  # PANE FALLBACK, and it is only a fallback: a pane can host a succession of sessions, so the
  # freshest beat carrying our pane is a good guess and never a proof. It is still safe, because the
  # freshness bound below is what actually gates arming — a guess that resolves to a retired
  # session's stale beat is REFUSED, not silently parked against.
  pane="${CC_PANE_ID:-${ITERM_SESSION_ID:-}}"; pane="${pane##*:}"
  [ -n "$pane" ] || return 1
  for f in "$BEAT_DIR"/*.json; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    [ "$(jq -r '.pane // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null)" = "$pane" ] || continue
    t="$(jq -r '.t // 0' "$f" 2>/dev/null)"; case "$t" in ''|*[!0-9]*) t=0 ;; esac
    if [ "$t" -gt "$best_t" ]; then best_t="$t"; p="${f##*/}"; best="${p%.json}"; fi
  done
  [ -n "$best" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$best"
}

_refuse() {  # <token> <what-to-do-instead>
  echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=refused reason=$1 uuid=${CUR_UUID} — NOTHING was parked, so no /goal deferral was created by this call. $2" >&2
  exit 6
}

if [ "$IDLE_SCOPED" = 1 ]; then
  # (0) THE ORACLE ITSELF. Without a readable, FRESH beat for this session there is no way to know a
  #     new turn happened, so C2 can never fire and this watcher would be an ordinary parked task
  #     wearing the safe mode's name — the starvation pole, armed on purpose. Fail closed.
  if [ "$_have_lib" != 1 ]; then
    _refuse no-mailbox-lib "The mode's refusals are evaluated over the WHOLE keyset (pane box AND session box), and without hooks/lib/mailbox-pending.sh only one key is visible. A partially-evaluated refusal is worse than none. Use the bare form under no goal, or repair the lib."
  fi
  SID="$(_resolve_sid 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [ -z "$SID" ]; then
    _refuse no-session-id "Pass --sid <your session id> (the hooks that instruct this arm interpolate it for you). Without it the beat file that proves 'a new turn happened' cannot be found, and this watcher could never stand itself down."
  fi
  _bt="$(_beat_field "$SID" '.t')"; case "$_bt" in ''|*[!0-9]*) _bt="" ;; esac
  if [ -z "$_bt" ]; then
    _refuse no-beat "No readable turn-boundary beat at $BEAT_DIR/$SID.json. hooks/session-beat.sh is what writes it, on UserPromptSubmit and Stop; if it is not wired in this config dir, the idle-scoped exit condition has no oracle and this mode must not be used here."
  fi
  _now="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  if [ "$(( _now - _bt ))" -gt "$BEAT_FRESH_S" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    _refuse stale-beat "The newest beat for [$SID] is $(( _now - _bt ))s old (bound ${BEAT_FRESH_S}s). An arm happens INSIDE a turn, so a live beat producer makes this seconds old — a stale one means either the producer is not running or the sid is not this session's."
  fi
  BEAT_BASE_SEQ="$(_beat_field "$SID" '.seq')"; case "$BEAT_BASE_SEQ" in ''|*[!0-9]*) BEAT_BASE_SEQ=0 ;; esac
  # THE ALLOWANCE, and why the doc's "exclude Stop-kind beats" is implemented as an OFFSET rather
  # than a kind filter. The beat file holds only the LATEST boundary, so a filter on `kind` alone has
  # a sampling hole: a whole turn can complete inside one poll interval, leaving us looking at a
  # Stop-kind beat whose predecessor we never saw — and a watcher that ignores it stays parked over a
  # session that HAS moved on. That is the starvation pole re-entering through the oracle. The offset
  # closes it: the arm turn's own trailing Stop (baseline+1) is excluded, and nothing else is.
  # Erring toward standing down early costs one bounce turn; erring the other way costs the goal.
  #
  # 🚨 THE ALLOWANCE IS UNCONDITIONAL, and it used to be gated on `kind = prompt` — which made this
  # mode a DETERMINISTIC NO-OP on the one path that arms it (backlog b60eb29e97dd; measured 2/2,
  # `beat seq > 3` and `beat seq > 6`, each watcher standing down at the very Stop that ended the
  # turn it was armed in). The gate encoded "turns alternate prompt/stop, so a sample taken inside a
  # turn is that turn's prompt beat". THE SITE THAT DEMANDS THE ARM DOES NOT PRODUCE ONE.
  # hooks/session-continue.sh's wake floor reaches the model by BLOCKING the Stop, and a Stop-hook
  # continuation turn fires NO UserPromptSubmit: cli.js turns the blocking error into a user message
  # directly (`d6({content: getStopHookMessage(...), isMeta:true})`) and the query loop `continue`s
  # with `stopHookActive:true`, never re-entering processUserInput, the sole caller of
  # executeUserPromptSubmitHooks. So hooks/session-beat.sh writes no prompt beat for that turn, the
  # newest beat at arm time is the PREVIOUS turn's Stop, the gate read `kind = stop`, the allowance
  # stayed 0, and the arm turn's own trailing Stop at baseline+1 tripped C2 seconds later. The wake
  # floor then burned its 2-attempt budget re-instructing an arm that could not survive being armed,
  # and the session went idle DEAF — the exact failure the floor exists to prevent.
  # (The other two instructing sites land on different turn shapes — hooks/mailbox-drain.sh nudges
  # via UserPromptSubmit additionalContext, so ITS arm turn does open with a prompt beat, and the
  # hooks/validate-bash.sh denial arrives as a tool_result inside whichever turn is running. An
  # oracle that has to be right on all three cannot be keyed on the arm turn's opening beat at all.)
  # The invariant that actually holds is kind-independent: AT MOST ONE boundary beat can be written
  # by the arm turn after the arm, its own trailing Stop, because a turn's opening beat (when it has
  # one at all) precedes the arm. So tolerate exactly one, always. A `prompt`-kind beat above the
  # baseline is never the arm turn's own and still stands down at +1, via the second clause of
  # _turn_moved below — which is what keeps a queued operator prompt, or a task notification, from
  # riding the allowance. Evidence: docs/research/idle-scoped-arm-turn-2026-08-27.md.
  BEAT_STOP_ALLOWANCE=1
  # (C4) MAIL ALREADY PENDING = you have work. Arming here would defer the judgment of state the
  #      session already holds, which is the deferral this mode exists to make impossible.
  for _k in $(_keys); do
    if mailbox_has_pending "$_k" 2>/dev/null; then
      _refuse pending-mail "Unread mail is waiting on key [$_k]. Read it (it surfaces at your next boundary, or: cc-mail), act on it, and arm only once your inbox is quiet — a watcher armed over pending mail defers a /goal evaluation of state you are already holding."
    fi
  done
  # (C3) SINGLE INSTANCE. Overlapping awaiters are how a deferral outlives the idle that justified
  #      it: watcher B keeps the registry non-terminal across the very wake that stood A down.
  for _k in $(_keys); do
    if _sib="$(_claim_live "$_k")"; then
      _refuse sibling-watcher "A live watcher (pid $_sib) already claims key [$_k]. It is your wake path; a second one would keep the task registry non-terminal across the wake that stands the first one down, which is exactly the permanent deferral this mode prevents."
    fi
  done
  echo "$_banner" >&2
  echo "cc-await-ping: idle-scoped — will also stand down on any new turn of [$SID] (beat seq > $(( BEAT_BASE_SEQ + BEAT_STOP_ALLOWANCE )))" >&2
fi

# ── C2: THE EXIT THAT MAKES THE DEFERRAL IDLE-SCOPED ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Consulted every poll, AFTER the mail check: if a ping and a new turn both happened, the ping is the
# thing the session must not lose, and delivering it also ends the watch.
_turn_moved() {  # 0 iff this session has taken a new turn since the arm baseline
  local seq
  [ "$IDLE_SCOPED" = 1 ] || return 1
  seq="$(_beat_field "$SID" '.seq')"; case "$seq" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  # A Stop-kind beat at exactly baseline+1 is the arm turn's OWN trailing stop — never a new turn,
  # whether or not that turn opened with a prompt beat (see THE ALLOWANCE at the arm gate).
  if [ "$seq" -gt "$(( BEAT_BASE_SEQ + BEAT_STOP_ALLOWANCE ))" ]; then return 0; fi
  # …but a PROMPT-kind beat at baseline+1 is not: a turn's opening beat precedes the arm, so this one
  # opened a turn that started after it. This is the clause that keeps a queued operator prompt or a
  # task notification from riding the allowance the arm turn's own Stop is owed.
  [ "$seq" -gt "$BEAT_BASE_SEQ" ] && [ "$(_beat_field "$SID" '.kind')" = "prompt" ] && return 0
  return 1
}

# ── THE SENDER RECORDER (backlog b38279c10c55) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The verdict below can say a watcher was killed but not BY WHOM, because an exit code carries no
# sender. `si_pid` does, and only a handler installed with SA_SIGINFO ever sees it.
#
# THE BUILD CONSTRAINT THIS FILE USED TO CARRY WAS FALSE. The comment below still says "an
# SA_SIGINFO recorder is the only thing that yields si_pid" — true — but the doc it points at then
# inferred that macOS shipping no `sigwaitinfo` left a COMPILED helper as the only route, which would
# mean a new file in bin/ and a live-layer add that is dead until the converger runs. `sigwaitinfo`'s
# absence says nothing about `sigaction`, which ctypes calls directly. So the recorder lives here, in
# an already-symlinked file, and goes live on the ordinary fast-forward.
# Measured twice (docs/research/exit-144-population-2026-08-19.md § UPDATE 2026-08-19): group TERM →
# si_pid = the group's killer; single-pid TERM → si_pid = the sender. Two Darwin specifics that a
# re-derivation gets wrong: sigset_t is a bare uint32 (so struct sigaction is {handler, uint32, int},
# NOT the glibc layout), and the CFUNCTYPE thunk must be held in a live variable or ctypes frees it
# and the handler segfaults on delivery.
#
# WHAT IT CAN AND CANNOT SEE — stated here so a reader does not over-read a silent verdict. The 144
# shape is a kill of the process GROUP, so the recorder, being a child in that group, is signalled
# too and names the sender. A 143 (this process alone) never reaches the recorder, so the sender
# stays unknown exactly as before. That asymmetry is the right way round: 144 is the shape under
# investigation.
#
# FAILS OPEN, ALWAYS. No python3, a spawn failure, a ctypes refusal — any of them leaves the watcher
# running precisely as it did before. A diagnostic side-car must never fail wider than itself.
# Kill-switch: CC_AWAIT_PING_SIGRECORD=0. Default ON, deliberately: measured cost is ~10.3 MB
# physical footprint per armed watcher (9 armed concurrently at the time of writing ⇒ ~93 MB), it is
# self-limiting because the diagnostic only has to catch one event, and an off-by-default recorder is
# one nobody ever turns on — inert by construction. NOTE the cost figure is footprint, not `ps` RSS:
# RSS reads 15.4 MB for the same process because it counts the interpreter's shared COW text once per
# mapper, the same overcount master 2029c52b8a32 names for this box.
SIGRECORD_PID=""
SIGRECORD_CAP=""
# The captured si_pid, kept as a global on purpose: _sigrecord_disarm `rm`s the capture file, and it
# runs BETWEEN _sigrecord_sender and the notice writers. Re-reading the file later would find
# nothing, and re-ordering the disarm would trade a diagnostic for the safety-critical cleanup.
SENDER_PID=""

# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — trap/startup only.
_sigrecord_arm() {
  [ "${CC_AWAIT_PING_SIGRECORD:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  SIGRECORD_CAP="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/cc-await-ping-sender.$$"
  rm -f "$SIGRECORD_CAP" 2>/dev/null || true
  python3 - "$SIGRECORD_CAP" "$$" <<'PYSIGREC' >/dev/null 2>&1 &
import ctypes, ctypes.util, os, signal, sys, time
try:
    libc = ctypes.CDLL(ctypes.util.find_library("c"), use_errno=True)
    OUT, PARENT = sys.argv[1], int(sys.argv[2])
    SA_SIGINFO = 0x0040

    class SIGINFO(ctypes.Structure):            # Darwin sys/signal.h
        _fields_ = [("si_signo", ctypes.c_int), ("si_errno", ctypes.c_int),
                    ("si_code", ctypes.c_int),  ("si_pid",   ctypes.c_int),
                    ("si_uid", ctypes.c_uint),  ("si_status", ctypes.c_int),
                    ("si_addr", ctypes.c_void_p), ("si_value", ctypes.c_void_p),
                    ("si_band", ctypes.c_long), ("__pad", ctypes.c_ulong * 7)]

    class SIGACTION(ctypes.Structure):          # sigset_t is a bare uint32 on Darwin
        _fields_ = [("sa_handler", ctypes.c_void_p), ("sa_mask", ctypes.c_uint32),
                    ("sa_flags", ctypes.c_int)]

    HANDLER = ctypes.CFUNCTYPE(None, ctypes.c_int, ctypes.POINTER(SIGINFO), ctypes.c_void_p)

    def _on_sig(signo, info, _ctx):
        i = info.contents
        try:
            with open(OUT, "a") as fh:          # append + fsync: we are already dying
                fh.write("sig=%d si_pid=%d si_uid=%d si_code=%d\n"
                         % (i.si_signo, i.si_pid, i.si_uid, i.si_code))
                fh.flush(); os.fsync(fh.fileno())
        except Exception:
            pass
        os._exit(0)

    _KEEP = HANDLER(_on_sig)                    # MUST outlive the call — ctypes frees the thunk else
    act = SIGACTION()
    act.sa_handler = ctypes.cast(_KEEP, ctypes.c_void_p)
    act.sa_mask, act.sa_flags = 0, SA_SIGINFO
    for _s in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT):
        libc.sigaction(_s, ctypes.byref(act), None)
    # Never outlive the watcher. The EXIT trap reaps us on an ordinary death, but a SIGKILLed watcher
    # runs no trap at all, and an orphaned interpreter sleeping forever is a ~10 MB leak into the very
    # footprint effort this row sits in. Poll the parent instead of trusting the reap.
    while True:
        if os.getppid() != PARENT:
            os._exit(0)
        time.sleep(5)
except Exception:
    os._exit(0)
PYSIGREC
  SIGRECORD_PID=$!
  return 0
}

# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — trap/startup only.
_sigrecord_disarm() {
  # KILL, not TERM, deliberately: the recorder handles TERM by writing a capture file and the `rm`
  # below would race that write, leaving a stray temp file naming US as the sender. By the time we
  # disarm we have already read anything we wanted, so there is nothing to flush.
  [ -n "${SIGRECORD_PID:-}" ] && kill -KILL "$SIGRECORD_PID" 2>/dev/null
  [ -n "${SIGRECORD_CAP:-}" ] && rm -f "$SIGRECORD_CAP" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

# Reads the captured sender, BOUNDED — this runs inside a signal handler, which must not block. The
# recorder fsyncs the moment it is delivered and both processes are signalled in the same instant, so
# 0.5s is generous; a 143 never reaches the recorder and correctly spends the whole bound returning
# nothing. Resolving the pid to a command is best-effort and uses `command=` (full argv), never
# `comm=`, which on Darwin reports the executable image rather than the work.
# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — its only caller, _sig_verdict, is itself trap-only.
_sigrecord_sender() {
  local n=0 line pid cmd
  [ -n "${SIGRECORD_CAP:-}" ] || return 0
  while [ "$n" -lt 10 ]; do
    [ -s "$SIGRECORD_CAP" ] && break
    sleep 0.05 2>/dev/null || break
    n=$(( n + 1 ))
  done
  [ -s "$SIGRECORD_CAP" ] || return 0
  line="$(head -n1 "$SIGRECORD_CAP" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$line" ] || return 0
  pid="$(printf '%s' "$line" | sed -n 's/.*si_pid=\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p')"
  [ -n "$pid" ] || return 0
  # NO GLOBAL IS SET HERE, and the temptation to set one is the trap: this function's ONLY caller
  # invokes it as `sender="$(_sigrecord_sender)"`, so every assignment it makes dies with that
  # command-substitution subshell. It would look wired and read empty. _sig_verdict re-parses the pid
  # out of the clause below instead — one source, and one that survives the subshell.
  cmd="$(ps -o command= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | head -n1 | cut -c1-120)"
  if [ -n "$cmd" ]; then
    printf ' SENDER IDENTIFIED: %s sender_cmd=[%s].' "$line" "$cmd"
  else
    printf ' SENDER IDENTIFIED: %s (sender already gone, no argv).' "$line"
  fi
  return 0
}

trap '_unbeat; _sigrecord_disarm' EXIT
_sigrecord_arm

# ── A KILLED WATCHER MUST NOT DIE SILENT (G10, backlog 5b77e20d9db6) ──────────────────────────────
# The EXIT trap above was the ONLY trap in this file, so an external SIGTERM ended the watch with no
# word at all: the wake path this session was relying on simply stopped existing, and the reader's
# whole account of it was the harness's `failed with exit code 143` / `144`.
#
# THIS DOES NOT PREVENT THE KILL, AND CANNOT. The sender is NOT recoverable from an exit code (see
# the header: 143 = this process alone was TERMed, 144 = the harness's sentinel for the process
# GROUP; an SA_SIGINFO recorder is the only thing that yields si_pid, and 144 proves only that the
# signal came from outside the harness). What it fixes is the SILENCE and the STALE CLAIM: the
# marker is cleared before dying, so a dead watcher stops advertising an armed wake path to
# cc-notify / mailbox-drain / cc-announce, and a `verdict=killed` line rides the stderr that the
# harness's task-completion notification actually carries — the same convention the timeout verdict
# at the foot of this file uses, and for the same reason.
#
# NOTE: backlog 5b77e20d9db6 reads 144 as 128+16 ⇒ SIGURG. That is WRONG on the signal (SIGURG's
# default action is IGNORE, so bash cannot die from it) though right in its conclusion that this is
# an external group-kill rather than a designed exit. Measured: `kill -TERM <inner pid>` → 143;
# `kill -TERM -<pgid>` → 144 with si_pid captured showing sig=15.
#
# Exits 128+signal deliberately: that is the number the harness already reports for the
# watcher-alone case, so the trap adds a verdict without inventing a code its readers must learn.
# Caveat kept honest — bash defers a trap until the current foreground command returns, so a TERM
# aimed at this process alone can be up to --interval seconds late. A group-TERM (the measured 144)
# also kills the `sleep`, so the trap fires promptly in exactly the case that produced the defect.
#
# ── F-2 (2026-08-09): SAY IT ON A CHANNEL THE LEAD ACTS ON, NOT ONLY THE ONE IT IGNORES ────────────
# MEASURED the same night as F-3: the lead's watcher exited 144 — which the header above proves is an
# external group-TERM — and THE LEAD DID NOT NOTICE IT HAD GONE DEAF. The text below is correct and
# says exactly the right thing; the problem is where it rides. stderr of a background task the harness
# renders as `failed with exit code 144` is a stream a lead triages as noise, and a lead must never
# infer its own liveness from its watcher's absence of complaint.
#
# So the verdict is ALSO written into the watched inbox itself — the one channel this whole substrate
# already proves a lead consumes. hooks/mailbox-drain.sh surfaces it at the next SessionStart /
# UserPromptSubmit / PostToolUse boundary as `📬 peer mail` additionalContext AND as an operator-visible
# systemMessage, so the human sees the wake path drop too. No new transport, no new convention: the
# line is the ordinary `<ISO> [<from>] <msg>` the box already holds.
#
# WRITTEN DIRECTLY, NOT VIA cc-notify: cc-notify resolves aliases and follows `.forward` chains, and
# the box we must reach is precisely the one WE were watching. A resolver could send this somewhere
# else — the failure mode would be a wake-path alarm delivered to a session that has no such watcher.
# Best-effort and never fatal: we are already dying, and a failed append must not cost the verdict on
# stderr or the marker cleanup. It is deliberately NOT filtered by the drain's fixture/noise grep, so
# it counts as real traffic and cannot be crowded out.
#
# ── E1 (2026-08-15): THE NOTICE MUST NOT PRESCRIBE AN ACT THE CHOKEPOINT DENIES ────────────────────
# docs/research/goal-safe-2way-comms-2026-08-13.md §8 E1, from the operator screenshot that opened
# that investigation. A session with a LIVE `/goal` killed its own pre-goal watcher — correct, and
# the doctrine's own instruction — and this notice then told it to RE-ARM, which is the exact shape
# hooks/validate-bash.sh:232 DENIES under a live goal (a parked background Bash makes CC skip goal
# evaluation at every Stop). Three defects in one frame: the arm existed to be killed, the kill read
# as `failed with exit code 144`, and the corpse's own advice pointed back at the denied act.
#
# WHY THE TEXT CARRIES BOTH BRANCHES RATHER THAN BRANCHING ON A PREDICATE HERE. This process cannot
# see the goal: `goal_live_condition` needs a transcript path, which a watcher is never handed and
# could only reach through a registry row → session id → project-slug lookup — i.e. a multi-MB grep
# run inside a SIGTERM handler, the one place in this file that must not block. And it would answer a
# READ-time question at WRITE time: this line then sits in the box until the next boundary drains it,
# by which point the goal may have been armed, met, or cleared. The reader is not left guessing
# either — hooks/mailbox-drain.sh evaluates the live predicate at that same boundary and emits its
# own goal-aware nudge in the same block (its E2 arm names a goal-blocking watcher's pid outright),
# so this line's whole job is to be CORRECT UNDER BOTH STATES rather than to contradict it.
#
# ── F5 (2026-08-22): NAMING THE CHECK IS NOT THE REMEDY THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE REFUSES ────────────────
# docs/research/handoff-high-value-capture-2026-08-19.md §5 F5, backlog 3078f45dded4. The refusal
# above is sound and STANDS — it refuses EVALUATING the predicate here (a multi-MB grep in a SIGTERM
# handler) and BRANCHING on it here (a read-time question answered at write time). It does not
# refuse NAMING the check, which costs one interpolated string and blocks nothing.
#
# The clause it does not defend is the last one: "the reader is not left guessing either". That was
# MEASURED FALSE. This function and mailbox-drain.sh's goal-aware arm landed in the SAME commit
# (e8c2435aa, 2026-08-17 00:02:05 -0700), and all six of the goal-hunt sessions the wave found START
# AFTER it — earliest +1 h 45 m — each having received this notice 4-12 times. So the drain-side arm
# was already live for every one of them and they went hunting anyway: the read-time evaluation
# exists, but nothing in this line TELLS the reader that the header they are looking at IS the
# verdict. The notice named a decision ("check before you act") and no check; the fix names both the
# zero-cost observable and the runnable predicate, and states its fail direction, because
# goal_live_condition returns rc 1 for "no goal", "met/failed" AND "could not read" alike
# (goal-state.sh header, FAIL DIRECTION) — a reader who treats rc 1 as a confident no re-creates the
# E1 defect from the other side.
#
# ── (B) vs (C) (2026-08-24): "YOUR WATCHER DIED" IS TWO EVENTS, AND ONE OF THEM IS AN EMERGENCY ───
# backlog 95fcadde830e (C, the dangerous class) and 0e0da162f77c (B, the benign one). The notice
# above classifies every kill identically, and its (a)/(b) branches are about the /goal, not about
# whether anything will re-arm. Two populations hide under one line:
#
#   (B) BENIGN — the watcher was torn down by its own arming session's RECYCLE EXIT. A successor is
#       already coming up on this pane and will arm its own watcher. Nothing is wrong.
#   (C) DANGEROUS — the arming session is STILL ALIVE and still taking turns, and its watcher died
#       anyway. Nothing re-arms. The session believes it is armed, every counter reads healthy, and
#       peer mail stops waking it. MEASURED on pane 102: SIGTERM at elapsed=770s of a 14400s budget,
#       si_pid resolving to a process verified ALIVE with the right cwd.
#
# THREE DISCRIMINATORS ARE REFUTED AND MUST NOT COME BACK.
#   1. REGISTRY IDENTITY (compare si_pid to the pane's own predecessor via cc-registry) — the cure
#      0e0da162f77c itself prescribes, and it has ZERO power. cc-registry is single-slot per pane, so
#      it only ever names the current occupant; both truth-table cells were measured and BOTH match
#      (B matched because the successor registered 10s after the kill; C matched because the live
#      arming session's own row precedes the kill). It hard-codes "nothing is wrong" for a match —
#      i.e. it prints the reassuring branch over exactly the dangerous class.
#   2. "DID THE SENDER BEAT AFTER THE KILL?" as a single question — structurally always 0 for
#      everyone. A cc-beats file is ONE ROW PER SESSION (a current-state record, not an append log),
#      so "count beats after t" reads BEFORE=1 AFTER=0 for a live sender and a dead one alike.
#   3. BEAT `kind` — refuted at n=1021. The 2026-08-25T03:02:10Z reboot left 1,010 sessions frozen at
#      kind=prompt (dead, killed without a clean stop) while 11 LIVE sessions also read kind=prompt.
#      `stop` is sufficient for a clean end; its ABSENCE means nothing.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES IS BEAT FRESHNESS, AND IT NEEDS A SECOND SAMPLE. A live sender's beat `t`/`seq`
# ADVANCES; a dead sender's is frozen. Two properties make it durable: the beat file is keyed by SID,
# so pid reuse cannot forge it, and it needs no knowledge of HOW the session died. But freshness was
# validated against a REBOOT — a privileged anchor ordinary operation does not give you — so the
# operational form is TWO READS SEPARATED IN TIME, never a comparison against some epoch and never a
# single read. That is what this code takes, and it is why the sampling cannot happen in the handler.
#
# WHY A DETACHED CHILD RATHER THAN THE SIGTERM HANDLER. The handler must not block: the disarm and
# the corpse notice are the safety-critical work and a killer may follow with SIGKILL. A second
# sample worth taking is tens of seconds away — a recycling `claude` is gone well inside that, a live
# one is trivially still there — so the handler spawns and returns, and the child appends its verdict
# when it has one. THE FAIL DIRECTION OF THAT CHOICE IS THE RIGHT WAY ROUND: in (B) the whole pane is
# being torn down and the child may die with it, costing a classification nobody needed (the
# successor re-arms regardless); in (C) the pane is alive by definition, which is exactly when the
# child survives to speak. It holds no descriptor of the harness's task pipe (</dev/null >/dev/null),
# so it can never keep a `run_in_background` arm reading non-terminal after the watcher is gone.
#
# WE DO NOT EXPLAIN THE REAP, AND DO NOT NEED TO. 95fcadde830e records (C)'s MECHANISM as unknown and
# whatever reaps the watcher as conditional. That is a separate row. Classifying (C) needs no
# mechanism at all: sender beat frozen and its process gone ⇒ (B); sender still alive, or beating
# after it killed you ⇒ (C). The notice's job is to say the correct thing, not to say why.
#
# THE BINDING IS si_pid → A BEAT ROW'S `.pid`, DIRECTLY, AND ITS LIMIT IS NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN.
# hooks/session-beat.sh records the CLAUDE-ANCESTOR pid, and the measured (C) case resolved si_pid to
# exactly that — a session process verified alive with the right cwd — so direct binding covers the
# population this row was opened on. A sender that is a mere DESCENDANT of a session (a hook shell, a
# `run_in_background` child) binds to nothing and lands in UNDETERMINED. That is a real gap, it fails
# toward deaf, and widening the binding by walking si_pid's ancestry is a separate row: the walk only
# answers while the sender is still alive, so it would add power in exactly one direction and needs
# its own evidence before it is trusted in the other.
#
# UNDETERMINED FAILS TOWARD DEAF. A sender that binds to no session beat cannot be told from a
# recycle teardown, and a 143 never reaches the recorder at all. The cost of a false "nothing is
# wrong" is a permanently deaf session; the cost of a false "you may be deaf" is one re-arm. So every
# branch this code cannot decide reads as (C).
CLASS_DELAY_S="${CC_AWAIT_CLASS_DELAY_S:-30}"
case "$CLASS_DELAY_S" in ''|*[!0-9]*) CLASS_DELAY_S=30 ;; esac

# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — the detached classifier is its only caller.
_beat_sid_for_pid() {   # <pid> → sid of the freshest beat whose .pid is this pid; empty if none
  local f b p t best="" best_t=0
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] || return 1
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  [ -d "$BEAT_DIR" ] || return 1
  for f in "$BEAT_DIR"/*.json; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    # PARSED, never grepped. hooks/session-beat.sh writes with `jq -n` and no `-c`, so these files
    # are PRETTY-PRINTED: a `grep '"pid":<n>'` returns 0 on a store that answers perfectly.
    p="$(jq -r '.pid // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
    [ "$p" = "$1" ] || continue
    t="$(jq -r '.t // 0' "$f" 2>/dev/null)"; case "$t" in ''|*[!0-9]*) t=0 ;; esac
    if [ "$t" -gt "$best_t" ] 2>/dev/null; then best_t="$t"; b="${f##*/}"; best="${b%.json}"; fi
  done
  [ -n "$best" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$best"
}

# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — the detached classifier is its only caller.
_pid_ident() {   # <pid> → its start time, TZ-PINNED; empty when the pid is gone
  # TZ=UTC on BOTH samples, deliberately: `lstart` renders in the ambient zone, so an unpinned pair
  # of reads straddling a DST flip would report the same process as a different one (memory
  # process-start-time-renders-in-ambient-timezone). Comparing OUR OWN two reads — never the beat
  # file's lstart, which some other process rendered in some other zone at some other time — is what
  # makes "same pid, same process" a claim about identity rather than about the clock.
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] || return 0
  TZ=UTC ps -o lstart= -p "$1" 2>/dev/null | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//'
}

# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — the detached classifier is its only caller.
_wake_class_line() {   # <sender pid> <key> — TWO reads CLASS_DELAY_S apart, then ONE verdict line
  local pid="$1" key="$2" box sid t1 s1 t2 s2 i1 i2 v why says
  box="$MAILBOX_DIR/${key}.md"
  sid="$(_beat_sid_for_pid "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  # ── SAMPLE 1 — taken now, milliseconds after the kill ──
  i1="$(_pid_ident "$pid")"
  t1="$(_beat_field "$sid" '.t'   2>/dev/null || true)"
  s1="$(_beat_field "$sid" '.seq' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  sleep "$CLASS_DELAY_S" 2>/dev/null || true
  # ── SAMPLE 2 — the whole point; a single read cannot separate "exiting" from "alive" ──
  i2="$(_pid_ident "$pid")"
  t2="$(_beat_field "$sid" '.t'   2>/dev/null || true)"
  s2="$(_beat_field "$sid" '.seq' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  case "$t1" in ''|*[!0-9]*) t1=0 ;; esac; case "$t2" in ''|*[!0-9]*) t2=0 ;; esac
  case "$s1" in ''|*[!0-9]*) s1=0 ;; esac; case "$s2" in ''|*[!0-9]*) s2=0 ;; esac

  local act=" Re-arm before you go idle — UNLESS a /goal is live in this session, in which case do NOT (branch (b) of the line above states why)."
  if [ -z "$sid" ]; then
    v="UNDETERMINED"; why="sender-binds-to-no-session-beat"
    says="pid $pid matches no cc-beats row, so it cannot be told from a recycle teardown. ASSUME YOU ARE DEAF: nothing is known to be coming to re-arm for you."
  elif [ "$s2" -gt "$s1" ] || [ "$t2" -gt "$t1" ]; then
    v="C-DEAF"; why="sender-beat-advanced-after-the-kill"
    # ${s1} BRACED, and it is load-bearing: `$s1→` takes the multibyte arrow INTO the variable
    # name, so it expands ${s1→} — unbound under `set -u`, which kills this whole child. It fails
    # SILENTLY too, because the child writes to /dev/null by design, so the only symptom is a
    # classification that never arrives. Found by running it, not by reading it.
    says="session [$sid] took a TURN after it killed your watcher (seq ${s1}→${s2}, t ${t1}→${t2}). It did not recycle, so NOTHING will re-arm: this session is DEAF to peer mail from now on."
  elif [ -n "$i2" ] && [ "$i2" = "$i1" ]; then
    v="C-DEAF"; why="sender-process-survived-its-own-kill"
    says="session [$sid] (pid $pid) is STILL RUNNING ${CLASS_DELAY_S}s after it killed your watcher — a recycle exit would be gone. NOTHING will re-arm: this session is DEAF to peer mail from now on."
  else
    v="B-BENIGN"; why="sender-session-exited-beat-frozen"
    says="session [$sid] EXITED and its beat is frozen (seq $s1, t $t1, unchanged across both samples; pid $pid gone). That is a recycle teardown — the successor coming up on this pane arms its own watcher, and nothing here is wrong."
    act=""   # the ONE branch where "nothing is wrong" is earned; it must not also demand an act
  fi

  mkdir -p "$MAILBOX_DIR" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  printf '%s [cc-await-ping] WAKE-PATH-CLASS: verdict=%s reason=%s — follow-up to the WAKE-PATH-DOWN line above: the watcher armed for [%s] was killed by pid %s, and TWO reads of that sender %ss apart (never one read, and never the beat kind field) say: %s%s\n' \
    "$(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z' 2>/dev/null)" "$v" "$why" "$key" "$pid" "$CLASS_DELAY_S" "$says" "$act" \
    >> "$box" 2>/dev/null || true
  return 0
}

# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — its only caller, _sig_verdict, is itself trap-only.
_wake_class_spawn() {   # fire-and-forget; MUST return instantly (we are inside a signal handler)
  [ "${CC_AWAIT_CLASSIFY:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0
  [ -n "${SENDER_PID:-}" ] || return 0
  [ -n "${CUR_UUID:-}" ] || return 0
  # `trap ''` so the teardown that is killing us cannot also take the sampler; </dev/null >/dev/null
  # so the child holds no descriptor of the harness's task pipe and cannot keep a backgrounded arm
  # reading non-terminal after the watcher is gone.
  ( trap '' HUP TERM INT
    _wake_class_line "$SENDER_PID" "$CUR_UUID"
  ) </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
  return 0
}

# ONE LINE, no embedded newline: the mailbox is line-oriented and a line IS a message.
# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — its only caller, _sig_verdict, is itself trap-only.
_wake_down_notice() {   # <signame> [<sender clause, already space-prefixed>]
  local box="$MAILBOX_DIR/${CUR_UUID}.md" cls
  [ -n "${CUR_UUID:-}" ] || return 0
  mkdir -p "$MAILBOX_DIR" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  # WHETHER THIS KILL IS BENIGN IS A FACT THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET — see the (B) vs (C) header above.
  # It takes two reads separated in time, so this line can only announce the pending verdict and name
  # the safe assumption to hold until it lands. Both texts fail toward DEAF.
  # THREE STATES, NOT TWO. "Disabled" and "no sender" converge on the same advice and must still be
  # told apart: a line that blames a missing si_pid for an operator's kill-switch is a false reason,
  # and a false reason is what sends the next reader hunting for a recorder failure that never
  # happened (the same defect the elapsed>=budget arm above was built to end).
  if [ -z "${SENDER_PID:-}" ]; then
    cls=" NO (B)-vs-(C) CLASSIFICATION IS POSSIBLE FOR THIS KILL: no si_pid was captured, so a benign recycle teardown cannot be told from a sender that is still alive and re-arms nothing. ASSUME THE DANGEROUS READING — you are deaf now."
  elif [ "${CC_AWAIT_CLASSIFY:-1}" = 0 ]; then
    cls=" NO (B)-vs-(C) CLASSIFICATION WILL FOLLOW: the sender IS known, but classification is switched off here (CC_AWAIT_CLASSIFY=0). ASSUME THE DANGEROUS READING — you are deaf now."
  else
    cls=" WHETHER THIS IS BENIGN IS BEING SAMPLED RIGHT NOW: a WAKE-PATH-CLASS line lands in this box in ~${CLASS_DELAY_S}s, classifying the kill as (B) your own arming session's RECYCLE EXIT — a successor is coming up and will re-arm, nothing is wrong — or (C) a sender that is STILL ALIVE and still taking turns, which re-arms nothing and leaves this session DEAF from now on. It decides on SENDER-BEAT FRESHNESS across two reads separated in time (registry identity, the beat kind field, and any single read are all measured-refuted discriminators). UNTIL THAT LINE ARRIVES, ASSUME (C): you are deaf now."
  fi
  # BOTH improvements, deliberately: trunk's MEASURED slice (elapsed + budget, so a reader can size
  # how much of the arm was actually covered) AND this commit's goal-aware branch (which repair
  # applies at all). They were written against each other and neither subsumes the other.
  printf '%s [cc-await-ping] WAKE-PATH-DOWN: the watcher armed for [%s] was TERMINATED from outside (SIG%s; %s budget=%ss — the MEASURED slice of the term it actually held) — it did NOT time out and consumed no ping.%s WHICH REPAIR APPLIES DEPENDS ON YOUR /goal, so check before you act. (a) NO live /goal → you are DEAF: nothing will wake this session on peer mail until you RE-ARM. Do it before you go idle: %s . (b) A /goal IS LIVE → do NOT re-arm. Claude Code skips /goal evaluation at every Stop where a non-terminal background Bash exists, so re-arming would silently disable the goal driving this session — hooks/validate-bash.sh denies that arm for exactly this reason. You are not deaf without it: the goal blocks your stops, so you keep taking turns and hooks/mailbox-drain.sh delivers peer mail at every boundary the goal forces. The goal-safe cross-turn lever is %s/.claude/hooks/session-continue.sh set "<next step>". THE CHECK, if you cannot tell which branch you are in: the drain that delivered this line re-evaluates goal-liveness at that same boundary, so the header above it IS the verdict — "no inbox wake path armed" is branch (a), and a nudge quoting your /goal condition is branch (b). To run the predicate yourself: . %s/.claude/hooks/lib/goal-state.sh && goal_live_condition <your transcript .jsonl> — it FAILS CLOSED, so rc 1 means "no live goal OR could not tell", never a confident no. Detail: docs/research/goal-safe-2way-comms-2026-08-13.md §8 E1; F5 in docs/research/handoff-high-value-capture-2026-08-19.md.%s\n' \
    "$(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z' 2>/dev/null)" "$CUR_UUID" "$1" "$(_elapsed_f)" "$TIMEOUT" "${2:-}" \
    "$HOME/.claude/bin/cc-await-ping --timeout 14400 --interval 15" "$HOME" "$HOME" "$cls" \
    >> "$box" 2>/dev/null || true
  return 0
}
# A SIGNAL THAT ARRIVES AFTER THE TERM EXPIRED IS NOT A KILL — it collected a watch that was already
# over. The wait loop only re-tests `elapsed < TIMEOUT` once per --interval (15s by default), and bash
# additionally defers a trap until the current foreground command returns, so there is a window of up
# to one interval in which the budget has been spent but the loop has not yet noticed. A signal
# landing in that window took the TERM path and emitted, verbatim, "it did NOT time out" — about a
# watcher whose own measured elapsed had already reached its budget.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-21 over the 552 WAKE-PATH-DOWN lines in ~/.claude/mailbox (271 carry a parseable
# elapsed/budget pair): 19 of them, 7%, report elapsed >= budget. Every one of those asserts something
# false about itself, and it needs no killer to explain — the successor reading it goes looking for an
# external process that does not exist.
#
# UNMEASURABLE STAYS A KILL. _elapsed_s returns -1 when no clock could answer, and -1 is never >=
# TIMEOUT, so an unknown elapsed falls through to the kill branch rather than being laundered into a
# clean timeout. Missing data is not evidence the term completed (memory lookup-miss-is-not-absence).
#
# EACH trap-only function carries its OWN directive, and that is not duplication. A `shellcheck
# disable` applies to the NEXT construct, so inserting this function below _sig_verdict's shared
# directive silently ORPHANED it: the comment covered the new arrival and _sig_verdict lost its
# cover, reddening the land gate's shellcheck arm on a function nobody had touched. The directive
# binds to position, so anything inserted between it and its subject steals it.
# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — its only caller, _sig_verdict, is itself trap-only.
_term_expired() {   # 0 = the budget was already spent when the signal arrived
  local e; e="$(_elapsed_s)"
  [ "$e" -ge 0 ] 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [ "$e" -ge "$TIMEOUT" ] 2>/dev/null || return 1
  return 0
}
# shellcheck disable=SC2329  # invoked indirectly — the three `trap` lines below are its only callers.
_sig_verdict() {   # <signame> <signum>
  local sender
  trap - TERM HUP INT EXIT
  _unbeat
  if _term_expired; then
    # The DESIGNED end of a watch, reached a beat before the loop could say so. Same disarm and the
    # same re-arm advice as any other end of term — but no anomaly, no "terminated from outside", and
    # no inbox alarm asking the successor to hunt for a culprit. Exit 2 is the file's documented
    # timeout status (see the Exit map at the top), NOT 128+signum: the outcome is what the watch
    # actually reached, and a reader keying on the exit code must not have to know which of two racing
    # clocks won.
    _sigrecord_disarm
    echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=timeout term=full $(_elapsed_f) budget=${TIMEOUT}s uuid=${CUR_UUID} — SIG$1 arrived AFTER the budget was already spent, so this is the designed end of the watch, not a kill: the wait loop re-tests only once per interval and had not yet returned. No ping was consumed and nothing is still claiming the wake path. If you are going idle, RE-ARM — unless a /goal is live in this session (a parked background Bash makes CC skip goal evaluation at every Stop)." >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  # _unbeat first: clearing the stale claim is the safety-critical half and is fast. The bounded
  # sender read comes after, which also gives the recorder those few extra milliseconds to land.
  sender="$(_sigrecord_sender)"
  # The pid comes back through the CLAUSE, not through a global — see _sigrecord_sender's note. The
  # capture file is gone one line below, so this is the last moment it can be read at all.
  SENDER_PID="$(printf '%s' "$sender" | sed -n 's/.*si_pid=\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p')"
  _sigrecord_disarm
  _wake_down_notice "$1" "$sender"
  # AFTER the notice, never before: the corpse line is the guaranteed half and must not wait on a
  # spawn. This returns instantly — the sampling happens in the detached child.
  _wake_class_spawn
  echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=killed signal=SIG$1 $(_elapsed_f) budget=${TIMEOUT}s uuid=${CUR_UUID} — this watcher was TERMINATED from outside; it did NOT time out and no ping was consumed. The wake path for [$CUR_UUID] is now DOWN and its .watching marker has been cleared, so nothing is still claiming it. A WAKE-PATH-DOWN line has also been written into this inbox, so the next boundary surfaces it as ordinary peer mail rather than leaving the verdict on a stream the harness labels 'failed'.${sender:- The sender was not captured: a kill of this process alone never reaches the recorder, and the recorder may also be disabled or unavailable — an exit code carries no sender either way.} If you are going idle, RE-ARM — UNLESS a /goal is live in this session, in which case do NOT (a parked background Bash makes CC skip goal evaluation at every Stop; the goal forces the turns that drain your mail). The inbox line states both branches." >&2
  exit $(( 128 + $2 ))
}
trap '_sig_verdict TERM 15' TERM
trap '_sig_verdict HUP 1'   HUP
trap '_sig_verdict INT 2'   INT

# ── OWNER GUARD — a watcher must never outlive the session it wakes ───────────────────────────────
# MEASURED (2026-07-29): a watcher whose owner dies is reparented to pid 1 and keeps polling. When
# mail arrives it takes it with ack_now=1 — advancing BOTH cursors — and prints the body to a stdout
# no model will ever read. The line is then marked PROVABLY CONSUMED for a session that no longer
# exists: cc-inbox-guard sees unacked=0 and never alarms, a read receipt reports `read`, and the
# successor's SessionStart adoption (which migrates from .acked) inherits nothing. A silent loss
# wearing the costume of a successful delivery — the exact failure this whole channel exists to end,
# re-created by the thing that closes it. The wake floor arms EVERY session, so this went from a rare
# hand-armed case to the fleet default; the guard has to land with it.
#
# THE OWNER ORACLE IS THE REGISTRY PID, NOT $PPID. Reparenting is NOT evidence: a legitimate
# `run_in_background` arm has its launching shell exit immediately, so a healthy watcher's ppid
# becomes 1 exactly like an orphan's (verified 2026-07-29). Keying on that would disarm the entire
# fleet. The registry row is what cc-notify already treats as authoritative for the same question.
#
# THAT PREMISE HAS SINCE INVERTED — AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES ANYWAY. Re-measured 2026-08-07 on CC
# 2.1.220: 9 of 9 live watchers across the fleet had a LIVE `/bin/zsh -c source <snapshot>` parent, so
# a healthy watcher's ppid is now the wrapper, not 1. Both readings are dated and both were true when
# taken; what does not change is that ppid answers a question we are not asking. It was once a false
# POSITIVE for death (healthy watchers looked orphaned) and is now a false NEGATIVE (a genuinely
# orphaned watcher can keep a live wrapper), so the registry-pid oracle is right for a strictly
# stronger reason than when it was written. Left as a worked example of why a "(verified)" claim in a
# comment needs its date carried with it. See docs/research/await-ping-exit-144-2026-08-07.md §6.
#
# FAIL-SAFE DIRECTION: only a PRESENT row naming a DEAD pid proves death. No row (an unregistered
# pane is legitimate), an unreadable row, or a live pid → keep watching. So the guard can strand a
# watcher it cannot adjudicate, but it can never kill a live session's wake path — and an
# unadjudicable watcher is still bounded by --timeout, which is the case that was always true.
#
# ── A CACHED PID IS NOT ENOUGH: EXIT 5 NOW NEEDS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT ORACLE (G10, 2026-08-08) ───
# MEASURED (backlog a116d60af388): this guard exited 5 announcing pane 700 was GONE while pane 700
# was demonstrably alive and typing. The pane id had been renumbered underneath the registry, so
# `<uuid>.json` named a pid that really was dead while the session it addressed was not — and a
# sibling `handoff-fire self-close` saw the SAME cwd under a DIFFERENT pane id. Nothing about the
# fail-safe direction above was wrong; the fault is that ONE cached field carried a verdict this
# destructive on its own. Exit 5 abandons a live session's wake path and leaves it deaf.
#
# So the pid oracle is now necessary and NOT sufficient. Once it fires, a corroborator must
# independently agree, and it must not be a second reader of the same row — that would prove nothing
# beyond what the first read already said. Two are consulted, in order:
#
#   B1  PANE LIVENESS — is $CUR_UUID in `it2 session list --json`? iTerm2's OWN answer about its own
#       panes, which touches the registry not at all. Catches the in-place case: the pane is right
#       there and only the recorded pid has been superseded.
#   B2  CWD OCCUPANCY — is the row's cwd held by a LIVE pid in a DIFFERENT registry row? That is the
#       renumbering signature the incident actually recorded, and it is the case B1 cannot see:
#       when the id itself changed, the old id is genuinely absent from iTerm2 while the session
#       that owned it is alive under the new one. Different field, different rows, different pids.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED ON UNKNOWN, exactly as cc-reconcile's prune is (:258): a transport that cannot answer
# means pane liveness is UNKNOWN, never "every pane is gone". So where it2 is absent or wedged, exit
# 5 becomes unreachable and the watcher falls back to being bounded by --timeout — the same
# unadjudicable-but-safe state the paragraph above already accepted. This change only ever makes
# exit 5 HARDER to reach; there is no input for which it now fires and previously did not.
#
# COST: the corroborators run ONLY after the pid oracle has fired, which is rare — and a veto is
# remembered for CC_AWAIT_OWNER_RECHECK_S so a permanently-stale row cannot turn a 15s poll into a
# 15s it2 IPC loop. The it2 call is wall-clock bounded for the reason cc-reconcile bounds its own:
# on the 2026-07-26 machine-wide wedge a bare `it2 session list --json` returned rc 124 with zero
# output while blocked forks piled up across ~110 sessions.
REG_DIR="${CC_REGISTRY_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/cc-registry}"
# `${VAR-}` not `${VAR:-}`: set-but-EMPTY means "no pane transport" and is honored verbatim, so a
# test can run the UNKNOWN branch. A test that cannot turn the transport off cannot prove the
# fail-closed direction differs from the confirming one.
IT2_BIN="${CC_AWAIT_IT2_BIN-$HOME/.claude/bin/it2}"
IT2_TIMEOUT_S="${CC_AWAIT_IT2_TIMEOUT_S:-5}"
OWNER_RECHECK_S="${CC_AWAIT_OWNER_RECHECK_S:-120}"
_TO_BIN=""
for _c in "$(command -v timeout 2>/dev/null || true)" "$(command -v gtimeout 2>/dev/null || true)" \
          /opt/homebrew/bin/timeout /usr/local/bin/timeout \
          /opt/homebrew/bin/gtimeout /usr/local/bin/gtimeout; do
  [ -n "$_c" ] && [ -x "$_c" ] && { _TO_BIN="$_c"; break; }
done
unset _c

_it2_panes() {   # echo the live pane-id set; rc 1 = the transport could not answer (UNKNOWN)
  [ -n "$IT2_BIN" ] && [ -x "$IT2_BIN" ] || return 1
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  local out ids
  if [ -n "$_TO_BIN" ]; then out="$("$_TO_BIN" -k 3 "$IT2_TIMEOUT_S" "$IT2_BIN" session list --json 2>/dev/null)"
  else                       out="$("$IT2_BIN" session list --json 2>/dev/null)"; fi
  [ -n "$out" ] || return 1
  ids="$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq -r 'if type=="array" then .[].id // empty else empty end' 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$ids" ] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$ids"
}

_pane_gone() {   # 0 = CONFIRMED absent · 1 = PRESENT (alive) · 2 = cannot answer (UNKNOWN)
  local ids
  ids="$(_it2_panes)" || return 2
  # DRAINED, not -q. The rc map on the line above is what makes this the worst direction of the
  # four: `&&` failing does not return "unknown", it falls through to `return 0` — CONFIRMED
  # absent. So the one spelling that fails on a MATCH converts "this pane is alive" into the
  # strongest possible claim that it is dead, about the very session doing the waiting.
  # Two-stage, so the floor is 37,121 B; the feed here is the ALREADY-EXTRACTED id list, measured
  # 37 B over 12 panes at 2026-08-26T21:53Z. Deeply latent — three orders of magnitude of margin —
  # and drained anyway, because latency is a property of today's pane count, nothing announces the
  # crossing, and the failure is unrecoverable in the direction it fails.
  printf '%s\n' "$ids" | grep -xF "$CUR_UUID" >/dev/null && return 1
  return 0
}

_cwd_occupied() {  # 0 iff the row's cwd is held by a LIVE pid in some OTHER registry row
  local rf="$REG_DIR/$CUR_UUID.json" cwd f p
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  cwd="$(jq -r '.cwd // empty' "$rf" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$cwd" ] || return 1
  for f in "$REG_DIR"/*.json; do
    [ -e "$f" ] || continue
    [ "$f" = "$rf" ] && continue
    [ "$(jq -r '.cwd // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null)" = "$cwd" ] || continue
    p="$(jq -r '.pid // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$p" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
    kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

_corrob_ok_until=0
_veto() {  # remember an ALIVE corroboration, say it once per window, and keep watching
  local now; now="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  _corrob_ok_until=$(( now + OWNER_RECHECK_S ))
  echo "cc-await-ping: registry pid for [$CUR_UUID] is dead, but $1 — NOT exiting 5, still watching (re-checking in ${OWNER_RECHECK_S}s)" >&2
  return 1
}

_owner_dead() {  # 0 iff the watched pane is PROVABLY gone — pid oracle AND an independent corroborator
  local rf="$REG_DIR/$CUR_UUID.json" p now pg
  [ -f "$rf" ] || return 1
  p="$(jq -r '.pid // empty' "$rf" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$p" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null && return 1
  # ORACLE A has fired. It is necessary, not sufficient — corroborate or keep watching.
  now="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  [ "$now" -lt "$_corrob_ok_until" ] && return 1        # a recent corroborator said ALIVE; don't re-ask
  _pane_gone; pg=$?
  case "$pg" in
    1) _veto "iTerm2 still lists that pane as LIVE (a superseded pid, not a dead session)"; return $? ;;
    2) _veto "pane liveness is UNKNOWN (no readable it2 transport) — an unreadable transport is not 'every pane is gone'"; return $? ;;
  esac
  if _cwd_occupied; then
    _veto "its cwd is held by a LIVE session under a DIFFERENT pane id (the id was renumbered underneath the registry — a116d60af388)"
    return $?
  fi
  return 0
}

# ── F-3: THE TRIGGER IS PRIVATE, BECAUSE A SHARED CURSOR IS A STARVABLE ONE ───────────────────────
# MEASURED 2026-08-09 (docs/plans/TWO_WAY_SESSION_COMMS_PLAN.md § F-3). This watcher used to poll
# `mailbox_has_pending`, i.e. `lines - .seen`, and hooks/mailbox-drain.sh advances that SAME `.seen`
# on every delivery (its :13). So the two consumers raced for one cursor and whichever ran first
# starved the other. A peer executed the protocol perfectly — landed content-verified, pinged, then
# self-closed — the drain surfaced the ping at a UserPromptSubmit and advanced `.seen` past it, and
# the lead's armed watcher then polled an empty delta forever: `886.seen=3`, `886.acked=3`, 3 lines,
# alive and armed and permanently silent. It would have waited 4 hours for a ping already delivered.
#
# WHY "THE DRAIN ALREADY DELIVERED IT" IS NOT A FIX, and why this is not paranoia about a dup: a
# delivered message and a woken reader are DIFFERENT EVENTS. The drain's delivery is passive context
# inside an already-running turn; this watcher's delivery is an event that re-invokes an IDLE lead. A
# lead that goes idle after a drain-consumed ping is unreachable by both paths at once — the drain has
# nothing left to emit and the watcher has nothing left to see. That is the forever-hang.
#
# REJECTED ALTERNATIVES, both weaker than a private cursor:
#   • poll `.acked` instead — refuted by the post-mortem's own numbers: `.acked` was ALSO at EOF (3 of
#     3), so this watcher would have been equally deaf. It swaps coupling-to-the-drain for
#     coupling-to-the-Stop-fold rather than removing the coupling.
#   • have the drain signal a live `.watching` watcher — the drain only ever runs INSIDE the lead's own
#     session, so if it ran the lead is by definition already awake; and it buys a pid-signalling path
#     (with a recycled-pid hazard) to do what needs no IPC at all.
#
# So the watcher keeps its OWN cursor, seeded from `.seen` the first time it sees a key, and thereafter
# advanced only by ITS OWN takes. The seed is what preserves F6a (mail already pending when we arm
# still fires immediately); the privacy is what makes the drain unable to starve us. Dup-biased in the
# same direction the drain declares for itself: a duplicate wake is cheap, a lost wake is a 4-hour hang.
#
# bash 3.2 (macOS /bin/bash) has no associative arrays, so this is a newline-delimited "<key> <cursor>"
# string with a linear scan. The set is at most a couple of keys (lib → mailbox_keyset) and a mailbox
# key is validated to [A-Za-z0-9._-], so a space is an unambiguous separator. `while … done <<HEREDOC`
# does NOT subshell in bash, so _woke_set really does mutate the caller's WOKE_MAP.
WOKE_MAP=""
_woke_get() {   # <key> → print its cursor on stdout; rc 1 = not yet seeded
  local k="$1" line
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    case "$line" in "$k "*) printf '%s' "${line#* }"; return 0 ;; esac
  done <<WOKEEOF
$WOKE_MAP
WOKEEOF
  return 1
}
_woke_set() {   # <key> <cursor>
  local k="$1" v="$2" line out=""
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -n "$line" ] || continue
    case "$line" in "$k "*) continue ;; esac
    out="$out$line
"
  done <<WOKEEOF
$WOKE_MAP
WOKEEOF
  WOKE_MAP="$out$k $v"
}

# ── F12 (2026-08-22): A WATCHER MUST NOT READ ITS OWN PREDECESSOR'S CORPSE AS THE PING IT AWAITS ───
# backlog b1a9e3142ee4, NARROWED 2026-08-19 and red-proved here. `_wake_down_notice` writes the
# WAKE-PATH-DOWN verdict INTO the watched box on purpose (see its header: that placement is correct
# and must not move — the inbox is the one channel a lead provably consumes). But the take loop below
# decides "a ping arrived" purely on `mailbox_lines > cursor`, and a control line we wrote ourselves
# counts. So the arm that REPLACES a killed watcher can meet the dead one's notice, fire on it, and
# exit `verdict=ping elapsed=0s` — with harness status "completed, exit 0". The wake path was never
# established, and it is INDISTINGUISHABLE from one that delivered real mail. That polarity is the
# load-bearing half of the defect, and it is reached by the ordering the wake-floor itself prescribes
# ("arm before you stop" puts the arm in the same turn as the death, ahead of the boundary drain).
#
# WHY THE DISCRIMINATOR IS KEYED ON *OUR OWN KEYSET*, not on the marker text. A WAKE-PATH-DOWN
# FORWARDED from another pane is REAL PEER MAIL — a sibling telling us its wake path dropped — and a
# blanket skip of the marker would suppress it. The notice interpolates the key it was armed for, so
# "armed for [<one of OUR keys>]" separates our own corpse from a neighbour's report exactly.
#
# FAIL DIRECTION, deliberately OPEN: every uncertain branch below (unreadable box, empty window)
# returns "real mail present", i.e. the pre-fix behaviour. This suppressor can only ever make the
# watcher keep watching over lines it PROVED are its own; it can never swallow a delivery.
# ITS FOLLOW-UP IS THE SAME KIND OF LINE. The (B)-vs-(C) classifier appends a WAKE-PATH-CLASS line
# to the same box tens of seconds after the corpse notice — i.e. squarely inside the window where the
# REPLACEMENT watcher is already armed. Left out of this suppressor it would be the identical defect
# with a different marker: an arm meeting a control line WE wrote, firing `verdict=ping elapsed=0s`,
# and reporting a wake path that was never established. It carries the same `armed for [<key>]`
# clause, so the key-scoped rule below separates our own from a sibling's forwarded report unchanged.
_own_wake_down_line() {   # <line> → 0 iff this is a wake-path control line armed for one of OUR keys
  local _k
  case "$1" in
    *"[cc-await-ping] WAKE-PATH-DOWN: the watcher armed for ["*) ;;
    *"[cc-await-ping] WAKE-PATH-CLASS: "*"the watcher armed for ["*) ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  for _k in $(_keys); do
    case "$1" in *"the watcher armed for [$_k]"*) return 0 ;; esac
  done
  return 1
}
_real_mail_after() {   # <key> <from> → 0 iff the window holds >=1 line that is NOT our own control line
  local _f _line _n=0
  _f="$(mailbox_file "$1" 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  [ -n "$_f" ] && [ -f "$_f" ] || return 0
  while IFS= read -r _line || [ -n "$_line" ]; do
    _own_wake_down_line "$_line" || return 0
    _n=$(( _n + 1 ))
  done < <(tail -n "+$(( $2 + 1 ))" "$_f" 2>/dev/null)
  [ "$_n" -gt 0 ] && return 1
  return 0
}

elapsed=0
while [ "$elapsed" -lt "$TIMEOUT" ]; do
  next_uuid="$(resolve_uuid)"
  if [ -n "$next_uuid" ] && [ "$next_uuid" != "$CUR_UUID" ]; then
    _unbeat
    CUR_UUID="$next_uuid"; MBOX="$MAILBOX_DIR/$CUR_UUID.md"; baseline="$(_lc)"
    # A re-pointed role is a DIFFERENT logical inbox: drop the private cursors so the new key set
    # re-seeds from its own .seen on the next poll (same first-poll behaviour as a fresh arm).
    WOKE_MAP=""
    echo "cc-await-ping: role re-pointed → now watching $CUR_UUID" >&2
  fi
  mkdir -p "$MAILBOX_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true; _beat                                     # heartbeat

  # BEFORE any take: consuming is irreversible, so the orphan check has to gate it, not follow it.
  if _owner_dead; then
    echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=orphaned $(_elapsed_f) budget=${TIMEOUT}s — the session that armed me [$CUR_UUID] is GONE (registry pid dead) — exiting WITHOUT consuming its mail, so the lines stay unacked for cc-inbox-guard to alarm on and for the successor's adoption to inherit" >&2
    exit 5
  fi

  if [ "$_have_lib" = 1 ]; then
    # FIRST key of the set that has anything — not just our own. cc-notify writes the PANE box while
    # the drain reads the SESSION box (lib → mailbox_keyset), so checking one key is how a watcher
    # ends up deaf to a delivery sitting one filename away.
    _HIT=""; _FROM=0
    for _k in $(_keys); do
      # Seed this key's PRIVATE cursor from .seen the first time we see it — that is the F6a
      # behaviour (already-pending mail fires at once) and it is the ONLY time .seen is consulted.
      if ! _woke_get "$_k" >/dev/null 2>&1; then _woke_set "$_k" "$(mailbox_seen "$_k")"; fi
      _w="$(_woke_get "$_k")"; case "$_w" in ''|*[!0-9]*) _w=0 ;; esac
      if [ "$(mailbox_lines "$_k")" -gt "$_w" ]; then
        # F12: a window holding ONLY our own WAKE-PATH-DOWN corpse-notice is not a ping. Advance our
        # PRIVATE cursor past it (so we do not re-test it every poll) and keep watching. `.seen` is
        # deliberately untouched: the notice stays pending for hooks/mailbox-drain.sh, which is the
        # reader it was written for.
        if _real_mail_after "$_k" "$_w"; then _HIT="$_k"; _FROM="$_w"; break; fi
        _woke_set "$_k" "$(mailbox_lines "$_k")"
        echo "cc-await-ping: skipped $(( $(mailbox_lines "$_k") - _w )) WAKE-PATH-DOWN control line(s) of our own on [$_k] — not a ping, still watching (they stay unseen for the boundary drain)" >&2
      fi
    done
    if [ -n "$_HIT" ]; then
      # Window opens at OUR cursor, never at .seen — so a drain that already advanced .seen past this
      # line cannot turn our fire into an empty print (which is exactly what F-3 was).
      mailbox_take_from "$_HIT" "$_FROM" 1
      _trc=$?
      _woke_set "$_HIT" "$(mailbox_lines "$_HIT")"
      # F9: rc 2 = the body WAS printed but the cursor write FAILED — "the caller must escalate + still
      # deliver, never silently drop". This caller used to exit 0 regardless, so the lib's one escalation
      # contract had no honorer: a broken cursor looked exactly like a clean take. Deliver, alarm, exit loud.
      if [ "$_trc" = 2 ]; then
        # Through the comms-alarm chokepoint (stamps test_origin, diverts a fixture-context write out
        # of the operator's live root — see hooks/lib/comms-alarm.sh). Lib absent → the original
        # inline write, so this backstop never gets weaker than before the chokepoint existed.
        if command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
          _ca_rec="$(jq -nc --arg u "$_HIT" --arg ts "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null)" \
            '{kind:"cursor-write-failed", uuid:$u, actor:"cc-await-ping", reason:"mailbox_take delivered the body but could not advance .seen — the same mail will be re-delivered (dup, not loss); the guard cannot key on a cursor that is not moving", ts:$ts}' 2>/dev/null)"
          if command -v comms_alarm_write >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            comms_alarm_write cursor-fail "$_ca_rec" || true
          else
            _adir="${CC_COMMS_ALARM_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/autonomy/comms-alarms}"
            if mkdir -p "$_adir" 2>/dev/null; then
              printf '%s\n' "$_ca_rec" \
                > "$_adir/cursor-fail-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ 2>/dev/null)-$$-${RANDOM}.json" 2>/dev/null || true
            fi
          fi
        fi
        echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=ping-cursorfail $(_elapsed_f) budget=${TIMEOUT}s — ⚠ delivered, but the shared cursor could NOT be advanced for $_HIT — you will see this mail again (a dup, never a loss); alarm recorded" >&2
        exit 4
      fi
      # A DELIVERY IS ALSO A DURATION. The wake path's whole claim is that it was live between the arm
      # and the ping; `elapsed=` is the only place that interval is ever stated, and a take used to say
      # nothing at all — so a ping consumed 1 s after arming (an already-pending line, or a re-arm racing
      # a peer) was indistinguishable from one that proved a four-hour watch. stderr, never stdout: the
      # body on stdout is the payload and stays uncontaminated.
      echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=ping key=$_HIT $(_elapsed_f) budget=${TIMEOUT}s uuid=${CUR_UUID} — mail delivered; the wake path was live for that long before it fired." >&2
      exit 0
    fi
  else
    cur="$(_lc)"
    if [ "$cur" -gt "$baseline" ]; then
      tail -n +"$((baseline + 1))" "$MBOX"
      echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=ping key=$CUR_UUID $(_elapsed_f) budget=${TIMEOUT}s uuid=${CUR_UUID} — mail delivered (no-lib line-count path); the wake path was live for that long before it fired." >&2
      exit 0
    fi
  fi

  # C2 — the session moved on without us. Exit 0 SILENTLY: there is no body to deliver, and the
  # completion notification this exit rides is itself harmless to a session that is by definition
  # already awake. What it buys is the next natural Stop being registry-quiet, so the /goal judges
  # the NEW state exactly once instead of never (parked) or ninety times (bare).
  if _turn_moved; then
    echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=stood-down uuid=${CUR_UUID} sid=${SID} — this session took a new turn, so the idle window this watcher was scoped to is over. Nothing was missed and no ping was consumed: mail that arrives now surfaces at your turn boundaries, and any /goal you hold evaluates at your next stop. If you go idle again with an empty inbox, arm a fresh one." >&2
    exit 0
  fi

  sleep "$INTERVAL"
  elapsed=$((elapsed + INTERVAL))
done

# ── THE TIMEOUT IS A VERDICT, NOT A FAULT — SAY SO IN THE STREAM THAT SURVIVES ──────────────────
# rc 2 here means "the full watch elapsed with no ping", which is the NORMAL end of a healthy 14400 s
# arm. Its READER cannot tell: the Claude Code background-task classifier is `isError: exitCode !== 0`
# with a hardcoded per-basename exemption table compiled into the binary (grep's rc 1 is in it; there
# is no user-extensible entry), so the harness renders this as `failed with exit code 2` / status
# `failed` / telemetry `task_local_shell_failed`. A model re-invoked by that notification has triaged
# a clean timeout as a fault. We cannot change the classifier — but the notification carries STDERR
# (stdout is dropped; docs/research/mechanical-wake-asyncrewake-2026-07-29.md), so the discrimination
# can ride the stream the reader actually gets. Same `verdict=<token>` convention cc-notify (:294) and
# cc-announce use for exactly this reason: the exit code is for shells, the token is for readers.
#
# NOT remapped to exit 0. rc 2 is load-bearing for cc-wait (:138 branches on it) and is already
# overloaded with the usage errors at :108-109 — collapsing it would invert a contract and lose a
# distinction, to satisfy a classifier that would still be wrong about rc 3/4/5.
#
# ── AND THE DURATION IS MEASURED, SO `term=` CAN BE FALSIFIED (backlog e2903b01dfdc) ───────────────
# `elapsed=` is the wall delta from T0 (top of file), never $TIMEOUT — which is now reported beside it
# as `budget=`, correctly labelled as the thing the caller asked for rather than the thing that
# happened. `term=` is then a REAL discrimination between the two exits this path can be reached by:
#   full  — the watch blocked for at least its budget. The healthy end of a 14400 s arm.
#   short — the loop fell out having blocked for LESS. The counter driving the loop is a TICK
#           ACCUMULATOR (+INTERVAL per pass, :609) which assumes every `sleep` slept; a sleep that
#           returns early makes the accumulator run ahead of the clock, and the watch retires before
#           its term with the wake path disarmed — the collapse class backlog #127 records. A caller
#           who sees `short` was deaf for the difference and must RE-ARM.
# The point is not the token, it is that the token is DERIVED FROM A MEASUREMENT: the old line would
# have printed `elapsed=<budget>s` for a watcher that exited in the same second it started.
_E="$(_elapsed_s)"
_TERM=full
if [ "$_E" -lt 0 ]; then _TERM=unknown; elif [ "$_E" -lt "$TIMEOUT" ]; then _TERM=short; fi
_SHORT_NOTE=""
if [ "$_TERM" = short ]; then
  _SHORT_NOTE=" ⚠ term=short — this watch retired EARLY: it blocked for ${_E}s of a ${TIMEOUT}s budget, so the wake path was down for the remainder and this is an early exit wearing a timeout's clothes, NOT a completed watch. RE-ARM now, and if it repeats, the sleeps are being cut short (backlog #127's class)."
elif [ "$_TERM" = unknown ]; then
  _SHORT_NOTE=" ⚠ elapsed=unknown — no usable clock, so whether this watch held its term is NOT established by this line; treat it as unverified rather than as a full term."
fi
echo "cc-await-ping: verdict=timeout term=$_TERM $(_elapsed_f) budget=${TIMEOUT}s uuid=${CUR_UUID} — no ping arrived.${_SHORT_NOTE} A term=full outcome is the DESIGNED end of a watch that ran its term, not a failure: exit 2 here means 'nothing came', and the harness labels every non-zero exit 'failed'. Nothing is wrong and nothing needs debugging — if you are still going idle, RE-ARM (the watcher clears its own heartbeat on exit, so you are deaf until you do), UNLESS a /goal is live in this session: a parked background Bash makes CC skip goal evaluation at every Stop, so re-arming under a live goal disables the goal instead of protecting a wake path you do not need (the goal blocks your stops, and mailbox-drain delivers at every boundary it forces)." >&2
exit 2
